The '''2017 FA Cup final''' was an [[association football]] match between [[Arsenal F.C.–Chelsea F.C. rivalry|London rivals]] [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] and [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] on 27 May 2017 at [[Wembley Stadium]] in London, England. It was the [[List of FA Cup Finals|136th FA Cup final]] overall of English football's primary cup competition, the Football Association Challenge Cup ([[FA Cup]]), organised by [[the Football Association]] (FA).   This was a rematch of the [[2002 FA Cup Final]] and the first final since 2003 in which the sides had won once in the [[Premier League]] against one another, with a 3–0 victory for Arsenal in September 2016, and a 3–1 win for Chelsea the following February. The game was broadcast live in the United Kingdom by both [[BBC]] and [[BT Sport]]. [[BBC One]] provided the free-to-air coverage and BT Sport 2 was the pay-TV alternative.  

The match was [[Referee (association football)|refereed]] by [[Anthony Taylor (referee)|Anthony Taylor]] in front of a crowd of 89,472.  Arsenal [[Kick-off (association football)|kicked off]] and dominated the early stages, opening the scoring with a controversial goal from [[Alexis Sánchez]] in the fourth minute. On 68 minutes, [[Victor Moses]] fell in the Arsenal penalty area under pressure and appealed for a [[Penalty kick (association football)|penalty]] but instead was shown his second [[Penalty card#Yellow card|yellow card]] by the referee for [[Diving (association football)|diving]] and was [[Ejection (sports)|sent off]].  In the 76th minute, [[Diego Costa]] scored for Chelsea to level the score at 1&ndash;1: he received the ball from [[Willian (footballer, born 1988)|Willian]] and struck the ball past [[David Ospina]], the Arsenal [[goalkeeper (association football)|goalkeeper]].  Two minutes later [[Aaron Ramsey]] scored with a [[Header (association football)|header]] past Chelsea goalkeeper [[Thibaut Courtois]] after a [[Cross (association football)|cross]] from [[Olivier Giroud]], who had come on as a [[substitute (association football)|substitute]] less than a minute earlier, to make it 2&ndash;1 to Arsenal.  After four minutes of stoppage time, the whistle was blown and Arsenal won the FA Cup final 2&ndash;1, to secure a record-breaking 13th title, while [[Arsène Wenger]] became the most successful manager in the tournament's history with seven wins.

Winning the FA Cup would have meant Arsenal qualified for the [[2017–18 UEFA Europa League]] group stage had they not already secured their place in the competition after finishing fifth in the [[2016–17 Premier League]].  They also earned the right to play Chelsea who were the Premier League champions for the [[2017 FA Community Shield]].

