As a [[Premier League]] team, [[Arsenal F.C.|Arsenal]] started their campaign in the third round and were drawn away at [[EFL Championship]] club [[Preston North End F.C.|Preston North End]].<ref name="rules">{{Cite web | url = https://www.thefa.com/-/media/files/pdf/fa-competitions-2016-17/fa-cup/fa-cup-rules-2016-2017.ashx?la=en | format = pdf | title = Rules of the FA Challenge Cup Competition ("the Competition") | publisher = [[The Football Association]] | access-date = 7 January 2021 | page = 3 | archive-date = 27 March 2021 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20210327155507/https://www.thefa.com/-/media/files/pdf/fa-competitions-2016-17/fa-cup/fa-cup-rules-2016-2017.ashx?la=en | url-status = live }}</ref><ref name=deepdale/>  At [[Deepdale]], [[Callum Robinson]] put Preston ahead from close range in the seventh minute to give the home side a {{nowrap|1&ndash;0}} lead at half-time.  A minute after the interval, [[Aaron Ramsey]] equalised with a powerful shot from the edge of the Preston [[penalty area]] before [[Olivier Giroud]]'s deflected strike gave Arsenal a {{nowrap|2&ndash;1}} victory.<ref name=deepdale>{{cite web |last=Rose |first=Gary |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/38474710 |title=Preston North End 1–2 Arsenal |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=7 January 2017 |access-date=23 April 2017 |archive-date=6 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210106075155/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/38474710 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the fourth round, they faced fellow Premier League side [[Southampton F.C.|Southampton]] away from home at [[St Mary's Stadium]].  [[Danny Welbeck]] scored twice before the midway point of the first half before crossing to [[Theo Walcott]] to score from close range to make it {{nowrap|3&ndash;0}} at half-time.  Walcott completed his [[hat-trick#Association football|hat-trick]] in the second half, with two [[Assist (association football)|assists]] from [[Alexis Sánchez]], and Arsenal won {{nowrap|5&ndash;0}}.<ref>{{cite web |last=McNulty |first=Phil | authorlink=Phil McNulty| url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/38700749 |title=Southampton 0–5 Arsenal |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=28 January 2017 |access-date=23 April 2017 |archive-date=6 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210106075148/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/38700749 |url-status=live }}</ref> 

In the fifth round, Arsenal were drawn away against [[Non-League football|non-League]] side [[Sutton United F.C.|Sutton United]] of the [[National League (division)|National League]] who were 105 places below them in the [[English football league system]]. At Sutton's [[Gander Green Lane]], Arsenal won {{nowrap|2–0}} with goals from [[Lucas Pérez]] and Walcott either side of half-time.<ref>{{cite web |last=McNulty |first=Phil | authorlink=Phil McNulty | url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/38952752 |title=Sutton United 0–2 Arsenal |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=20 February 2017 |access-date=23 April 2017 |archive-date=6 January 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210106075154/https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/38952752 |url-status=live }}</ref> The match was also noted for Sutton United's reserve goalkeeper [[Wayne Shaw (footballer)|Wayne Shaw]] being investigated by [[the Football Association]] and [[Gambling Commission]]:  he had eaten a pie pitchside and admitted after the match that he had known that a betting company had offered [[fixed-odds betting|odds]] on him doing so.<ref>{{cite news |last=Aarons |first=Ed |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/feb/21/sutton-united-wayne-shaw-fa-betting-rules |title=Wayne Shaw leaves Sutton United amid investigations over pie-eating stunt |work=[[The Guardian]] |date=21 February 2017 |access-date=23 April 2017 |archive-date=23 November 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201123144832/https://www.theguardian.com/football/2017/feb/21/sutton-united-wayne-shaw-fa-betting-rules |url-status=live }}</ref> In the quarter-final, Arsenal were drawn at home at the [[Emirates Stadium]] against National League club [[Lincoln City F.C.|Lincoln City]].  Walcott gave Arsenal a one-goal lead in first-half [[stoppage time]] before second-half goals from Giroud, Sánchez and Ramsey, and an [[own goal]] by [[Luke Waterfall]], gave the home side a {{nowrap|5&ndash;0}} victory.<ref>{{cite web |last=Rose |first=Gary |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39162720 |title=Arsenal 5–0 Lincoln City |publisher=[[BBC Sport]] |date=11 March 2017 |access-date=23 April 2017 |archive-date=22 April 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170422235109/http://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/39162720 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the semi-final which took place at [[Wembley Stadium]] as a neutral venue, they played against fellow Premier League team [[Manchester City F.C.|Manchester City]].  After a goalless first half, [[Sergio Agüero]] put Manchester City ahead on the hour mark before [[Nacho Monreal]] scored the equaliser with a volley from [[Alex Oxlade-Chamberlain]]'s cross.  The match ended {{nowrap|1&ndash;1}} in regular time and went into [[Overtime (sports)|extra time]].  In the 101st minute, Sánchez scored from close range to put Arsenal ahead, a lead which they kept for a {{nowrap|2&ndash;1}} win and progression to the final.<ref name=euro>{{cite web |url=http://www.eurosport.co.uk/football/fa-cup/2016-2017/arsenal-v-man-city-preview-live-tv-details-tickets-match-facts-quotes-odds_sto6136780/story.shtml |title=Alexis Sanchez hits extra-time winner as Arsenal edge FA Cup thriller against Manchester City |publisher=[[Eurosport]] |date=17 February 2017 |access-date=23 April 2017 |archive-date=13 October 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191013141849/https://www.eurosport.co.uk/football/fa-cup/2016-2017/arsenal-v-man-city-preview-live-tv-details-tickets-match-facts-quotes-odds_sto6136780/story.shtml |url-status=live }}</ref>

